      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the sesame dipping sauce: combine ground sesame seeds, soy sauce, mirin, and rice vinegar in a small bowl. Stir well and set aside in small individual bowls."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the broth: place kombu in cold water and bring very slowly to just below a simmer. Remove the kombu just before boiling. Add sake."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Arrange the beef slices, cabbage, tofu, mushrooms, and spring onion on a large platter and bring to the table."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the pot in the centre of the table over a portable burner. Keep the broth at a gentle simmer. Add the harder vegetables first and let them cook for 3 to 4 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Each person takes a slice of beef with chopsticks and swishes it through the simmering broth for 5 to 10 seconds until just cooked. Dip in sesame sauce and eat immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Beef is turning grey and chewy",
              "cause": "It was cooked too long in the broth.",
              "fix": "Thin beef needs only 5 to 8 seconds. It should turn from red to just pink before you lift it out."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Broth becomes foamy and dirty-looking",
              "cause": "Proteins from the beef are coagulating on the surface.",
              "fix": "Skim the surface with a ladle every few minutes to keep the broth clear."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Sesame sauce is too thick",
              "cause": "Ground sesame absorbs the liquid quickly.",
              "fix": "Thin with a teaspoon of warm water or extra mirin until it has a smooth, pourable consistency."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Shabu-shabu is a Japanese hot pot built on restraint. The broth is almost plain. The beef does all the work. The technique came from Chinese hot pot but Japan made it much simpler and cleaner. High-end shabu-shabu restaurants in Tokyo use wagyu beef so finely marbled it cooks in seconds. At home, any well-marbled sirloin works beautifully."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
